  <!-- FAQ -->
  <h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2>

  <div class="faq-liste">
    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="Where is the best place to buy a used car in Australia?">
      <p>The common marketplaces are Gumtree, Facebook Marketplace and Carsales, plus dealer websites. Private sales are buyer beware, so a PPSR check before the purchase and an independent inspection matter a lot. Dealers add statutory warranties in some cases depending on the state and the vehicle, but the price is usually higher.</p>
    </AufklappBox>

    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="How do I transfer a car registration in New South Wales after buying?">
      <p>In NSW the buyer must transfer the registration into their name within 14 days. The seller lodges a notice of disposal in parallel. The transfer fee is 42 AUD within the 14 days and rises to 193 AUD afterwards. The online transfer only works if the seller has lodged the notice of disposal, you hold a NSW driver licence and the car is registered in NSW. Otherwise you do it in person at a Service NSW centre.</p>
    </AufklappBox>

    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="What is the PPSR check and why does it matter?">
      <p>The PPSR is a national register of security interests. A search for 2 AUD shows whether the car still carries finance or debt, and can also show if it is recorded as stolen or written off. It matters because private sellers do not have to disclose outstanding finance. If you buy a car with a debt on it and the previous owner stops paying, the finance company can repossess the car.</p>
    </AufklappBox>

    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="What does registration transfer and stamp duty cost for a used car in NSW?">
      <p>The transfer fee is 42 AUD within 14 days. On top comes vehicle stamp duty: for a value up to 44,999 AUD it is 3 AUD per 100 AUD, calculated on the higher of the purchase price or the market value. For us it came to 135 AUD, because the purchase price was 4,500 AUD and 3 AUD per 100 AUD makes exactly 135 AUD. For the next rego renewal, cars over five years old also need a safety inspection (pink slip, up to 52 AUD) and a compulsory green slip insurance whose price varies.</p>
    </AufklappBox>

    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="What should I watch for when registering a Queensland car in NSW?">
      <p>That is the expensive special case. A car from another state is not a simple transfer in NSW but a new registration as an interstate vehicle, in person at a Service NSW centre. Instead of the cheaper pink slip (52 AUD) you need a blue slip, an AUVIS inspection (82 AUD), you need new NSW plates and a new NSW green slip, and NSW stamp duty applies again. The interstate exemption only applies if the car was already registered in your own name and duty was already paid on it, not when you buy from a stranger.</p>
    </AufklappBox>

    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="Does a car in NSW need a safety inspection like a roadworthy or MOT?">
      <p>Most light vehicles older than five years need a safety inspection report, the pink slip, once a year before the rego renewal in NSW. It costs up to 52 AUD and is valid for six months. Only authorised inspection stations (AIS) can issue it. If the car has faults, you have to fix them before it passes, and that is where real repair costs can appear.</p>
    </AufklappBox>

    <AufklappBox lang="en" titel="How long can I drive in NSW on my overseas licence?">
      <p>As a temporary visa holder living in NSW, up to six months from arrival. After that you need a NSW licence to keep driving. If your licence is not written in English, you must also carry an English translation or an International Driving Permit while driving.</p>
    </AufklappBox>
  </div>

  <p><strong>As of:</strong> 2 August 2026. I flew out ahead, Lucy and the kids have since arrived. I will add the experience of the next rego and the repairs once the Jeep is through.</p>

